<!-- ═══ LEARN ═══ -->
<div class="sec on" id="sec-learn">

<div class="card">
<h2>🏛️ Before Electronic Computers — Ancient Calculating Devices</h2>
<p>Long before computers existed, humans invented tools to help with calculations. These early devices planted the seeds of modern computing.</p>
<div class="wiki-row"><div class="wiki-item">
<img src="./generation-timeline_files/Abacus_6.png" alt="A Chinese Suanpan abacus with beads" class="zoomable" data-author="Lookang" data-license="CC BY-SA 3.0" data-source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abacus_6.png" data-source-label="Wikimedia Commons">
<div class="wiki-item-cap">Abacus — ~2400 BC</div></div><div class="wiki-item">
<img src="./generation-timeline_files/Napier&#39;s_Bones_circa_1700,_Computer_History_Museum.jpg" alt="Napier&#39;s Bones — multiplication rods (1617)" class="zoomable" data-author="The wub" data-license="CC BY-SA 4.0" data-source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Napier%27s_Bones_circa_1700,_Computer_History_Museum.jpg" data-source-label="Wikimedia Commons">
<div class="wiki-item-cap">Napier's Bones — 1617</div></div><div class="wiki-item">
<img src="./generation-timeline_files/Arts_et_Metiers_Pascaline_dsc03869.jpg" alt="The Pascaline — mechanical adding machine by Blaise Pascal (1642)" class="zoomable" data-author="David Monniaux" data-license="CC BY-SA 3.0" data-source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arts_et_Metiers_Pascaline_dsc03869.jpg" data-source-label="Wikimedia Commons">
<div class="wiki-item-cap">Pascaline — 1642</div></div></div>
<div class="timeline">
  <div class="tl-item">
    <div class="tl-year">~2400 BC</div>
    <div class="tl-title">Abacus</div>
    <div class="tl-desc">The earliest known computing tool — rows of beads on rods. Used in China, Greece and Rome for arithmetic. Still used in some parts of the world today.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="tl-item">
    <div class="tl-year">1617</div>
    <div class="tl-title">Napier's Bones</div>
    <div class="tl-desc">John Napier invented numbered rods that could multiply and divide large numbers quickly. Also invented logarithms.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="tl-item">
    <div class="tl-year">1642</div>
    <div class="tl-title">Pascaline</div>
    <div class="tl-desc">Blaise Pascal built the first mechanical adding machine using gears and wheels. It could add and subtract automatically.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="tl-item">
    <div class="tl-year">1671</div>
    <div class="tl-title">Leibniz Calculator</div>
    <div class="tl-desc">Gottfried Leibniz improved Pascal's design to also multiply and divide. Introduced the binary number system (0s and 1s) — the language all computers use today.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="tl-item">
    <div class="tl-year">1801</div>
    <div class="tl-title">Jacquard Loom</div>
    <div class="tl-desc">Joseph Jacquard invented a loom controlled by punched cards — the first programmable machine. This directly inspired computer punched-card input systems.</div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="activity">
  <div class="activity-hd">🎯 Activity: Count Like an Abacus</div>
  <p>Use your fingers as abacus beads! Hold up all 10 fingers = 10. Each hand = one column. Try counting to 20 using both hands. How did ancient people use the abacus for larger numbers?</p>
</div>
</div>

<div class="card">
<h2>⚙️ Mechanical Era — Babbage &amp; Ada Lovelace</h2>
<div class="wiki-row"><div class="wiki-item">
<img src="./generation-timeline_files/Charles_Babbage_-_1860.jpg" alt="Charles Babbage — Father of the Computer" class="zoomable" data-author="Maull &amp; Polyblank" data-license="Public Domain" data-source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_Babbage_-_1860.jpg" data-source-label="Wikimedia Commons">
<div class="wiki-item-cap">Charles Babbage</div></div><div class="wiki-item">
<img src="./generation-timeline_files/Ada_Lovelace_portrait.jpg" alt="Ada Lovelace — World&#39;s First Programmer" class="zoomable" data-author="Alfred Edward Chalon" data-license="Public Domain" data-source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ada_Lovelace_portrait.jpg" data-source-label="Wikimedia Commons">
<div class="wiki-item-cap">Ada Lovelace</div></div><div class="wiki-item">
<img src="./generation-timeline_files/Babbage_Difference_Engine.jpg" alt="Babbage Difference Engine — reconstructed model at London Science Museum" class="zoomable" data-author="Geni" data-license="CC BY-SA 4.0" data-source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Babbage_Difference_Engine.jpg" data-source-label="Wikimedia Commons">
<div class="wiki-item-cap">Difference Engine</div></div></div>
<div class="cmp-grid">
  <div class="cmp-box blue">
    <div class="cmp-tag">1822 — Charles Babbage</div>
    <h3>Difference Engine</h3>
    <p>A mechanical machine designed to compute mathematical tables automatically using gears. Never fully built in his lifetime — but the design worked perfectly.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="cmp-box pur">
    <div class="cmp-tag">1837 — Charles Babbage</div>
    <h3>Analytical Engine</h3>
    <p>The first design for a general-purpose computer! Had a "mill" (CPU), "store" (memory), input (punched cards) and output — exactly like modern computers.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="cmp-box grn">
    <div class="cmp-tag">1843 — Ada Lovelace</div>
    <h3>First Algorithm</h3>
    <p>Ada Lovelace wrote the first step-by-step algorithm for the Analytical Engine to compute Bernoulli numbers — making her the world's <strong>first programmer</strong>.</p>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="def">💡 <strong>Babbage's Analytical Engine had all four key parts of a modern computer:</strong><br>
Input (punched cards) → Store (memory) → Mill (processor/CPU) → Output (printed results)</div>
</div>

<div class="card">
<h2>💡 Electronic Era — Generations of Computers</h2>
<div class="def">Computers are classified into <strong>5 generations</strong> based on the technology used to build them. Each generation was faster, smaller, cheaper and more powerful.</div>

<div class="gen-grid">
  <div class="gen-card" style="background:rgba(220,38,38,.06);border-color:rgba(220,38,38,.25)">
    <div class="gen-num">1</div>
    <div class="gen-era" style="color:#dc2626">1940s–1956</div>
    <div class="gen-title" style="color:#dc2626">First Generation</div>
    <div class="gen-tech">🔌 Vacuum tubes · Punched cards · Machine language · ENIAC, UNIVAC</div>
  </div>
  <div class="gen-card" style="background:rgba(217,119,6,.06);border-color:rgba(217,119,6,.25)">
    <div class="gen-num">2</div>
    <div class="gen-era" style="color:#b45309">1956–1963</div>
    <div class="gen-title" style="color:#b45309">Second Generation</div>
    <div class="gen-tech">🔧 Transistors · Assembly language · Magnetic core memory · IBM 1401</div>
  </div>
  <div class="gen-card" style="background:rgba(30,64,175,.06);border-color:rgba(30,64,175,.25)">
    <div class="gen-num">3</div>
    <div class="gen-era" style="color:#1e40af">1964–1971</div>
    <div class="gen-title" style="color:#1e40af">Third Generation</div>
    <div class="gen-tech">⚡ Integrated Circuits (IC) · High-level languages · IBM System/360</div>
  </div>
  <div class="gen-card" style="background:rgba(21,128,61,.06);border-color:rgba(21,128,61,.25)">
    <div class="gen-num">4</div>
    <div class="gen-era" style="color:#15803d">1971–Present</div>
    <div class="gen-title" style="color:#15803d">Fourth Generation</div>
    <div class="gen-tech">🖥️ Microprocessors (VLSI) · Personal computers · GUI · Intel, Apple</div>
  </div>
  <div class="gen-card" style="background:rgba(124,58,237,.06);border-color:rgba(124,58,237,.25)">
    <div class="gen-num">5</div>
    <div class="gen-era" style="color:#7c3aed">Present–Future</div>
    <div class="gen-title" style="color:#7c3aed">Fifth Generation</div>
    <div class="gen-tech">🤖 AI · Machine Learning · Natural Language Processing · Quantum Computing</div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="wiki-row"><div class="wiki-item">
<img src="./generation-timeline_files/Glen_Beck_and_Betty_Snyder_program_the_ENIAC_in_building_328_at_the_Ballistic_Research_Laboratory.jpg" alt="ENIAC — First general-purpose electronic computer (1945), University of Pennsylvania" class="zoomable" data-author="U.S. Army" data-license="Public Domain" data-source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eniac.jpg" data-source-label="Wikimedia Commons">
<div class="wiki-item-cap">ENIAC (1945) — 1st Gen</div></div><div class="wiki-item">
<img src="./generation-timeline_files/Bundesarchiv_B_145_Bild-F038812-0014,_Wolfsburg,_VW_Autowerk.jpg" alt="IBM mainframe computer in industrial use — 3rd generation era" class="zoomable" data-author="Bundesarchiv" data-license="CC BY-SA 3.0 DE" data-source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_B_145_Bild-F038812-0014,_Wolfsburg,_VW_Autowerk.jpg" data-source-label="Wikimedia Commons">
<div class="wiki-item-cap">IBM Mainframe — 3rd Gen</div></div><div class="wiki-item">
<img src="./generation-timeline_files/Computer_macintosh_128k,_1984_(all_about_Apple_onlus).jpg" alt="Apple Macintosh (1984) — first popular personal computer with GUI" class="zoomable" data-author="Sailko" data-license="CC BY-SA 3.0" data-source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Computer_macintosh_128k,_1984_(all_about_Apple_onlus).jpg" data-source-label="Wikimedia Commons">
<div class="wiki-item-cap">Apple Mac (1984) — 4th Gen</div></div></div>

<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Generation</th><th>Technology</th><th>Speed</th><th>Size</th><th>Cost</th><th>Language</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>1st (1940s)</strong></td><td>Vacuum tubes</td><td>Milliseconds</td><td>Room-sized</td><td>Very high</td><td>Machine language</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>2nd (1956)</strong></td><td>Transistors</td><td>Microseconds</td><td>Cabinet-sized</td><td>High</td><td>Assembly language</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>3rd (1964)</strong></td><td>Integrated Circuits</td><td>Nanoseconds</td><td>Desk-sized</td><td>Medium</td><td>COBOL, FORTRAN</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>4th (1971)</strong></td><td>Microprocessors</td><td>Picoseconds</td><td>Hand-held</td><td>Low</td><td>C, Python, Java</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>5th (now)</strong></td><td>AI/VLSI/Quantum</td><td>Femtoseconds</td><td>Wearable</td><td>Very low</td><td>AI/ML frameworks</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<div class="card">
<h2>🌐 Key Milestones Timeline</h2>
<div class="timeline">
  <div class="tl-item">
    <div class="tl-year">1945</div><div class="tl-title">ENIAC Completed</div>
    <div class="tl-desc">First electronic computer built at University of Pennsylvania. Filled 1800 sq ft, weighed 30 tons, used 18,000 vacuum tubes.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="tl-item">
    <div class="tl-year">1951</div><div class="tl-title">UNIVAC-I — First Commercial Computer</div>
    <div class="tl-desc">First computer sold commercially. Delivered to the US Census Bureau. Could process 1,905 operations per second.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="tl-item">
    <div class="tl-year">1956</div><div class="tl-title">Transistors Replace Vacuum Tubes</div>
    <div class="tl-desc">IBM 7090 used transistors — 100× faster, far smaller, much less power. Start of 2nd generation.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="tl-item">
    <div class="tl-year">1971</div><div class="tl-title">Intel 4004 — First Microprocessor</div>
    <div class="tl-desc">Intel's 4004 put the entire CPU on one chip — starting the microprocessor revolution and personal computing era.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="tl-item">
    <div class="tl-year">1984</div><div class="tl-title">Apple Macintosh — GUI for Everyone</div>
    <div class="tl-desc">First popular computer with a graphical user interface (windows, icons, mouse). Made computing accessible to non-engineers.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="tl-item">
    <div class="tl-year">1989</div><div class="tl-title">World Wide Web Invented</div>
    <div class="tl-desc">Tim Berners-Lee invented the Web at CERN to share research documents. Changed how the entire world communicates.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="tl-item">
    <div class="tl-year">2007</div><div class="tl-title">Smartphone Era Begins</div>
    <div class="tl-desc">Apple launches iPhone — a pocket-sized computer more powerful than 1990s supercomputers. Computing goes truly mobile.</div>
  </div>
</div>
</div>


<div class="card">
<h2>🇳🇵 History of Computer in Nepal</h2>
<div class="def">Nepal's journey with computers began in 1971 — relatively early for a developing nation — and has rapidly accelerated since the 1990s with internet access, mobile computing and now digital government services.</div>

<div class="timeline">
  <div class="tl-item">
    <div class="tl-year">1971</div>
    <div class="tl-title">First Computer in Nepal — IBM 1401</div>
    <div class="tl-desc">Nepal's first computer, an <strong>IBM 1401</strong> (2nd generation, transistor-based), was installed at the <strong>Central Bureau of Statistics (CBS)</strong> in Kathmandu. It was used for processing census data. Nepal was among the first countries in South Asia to use a computer for government work.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="tl-item">
    <div class="tl-year">1975</div>
    <div class="tl-title">Computer at Tribhuvan University</div>
    <div class="tl-desc">Tribhuvan University (TU) acquired computers for academic and research purposes. This marked the beginning of computer education in Nepal's higher education system.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="tl-item">
    <div class="tl-year">1980s</div>
    <div class="tl-title">Computers in Banking &amp; Government</div>
    <div class="tl-desc">Nepal Rastra Bank and major commercial banks began using computers for accounting and transaction records. Government ministries started computerising administrative records. Computers remained expensive and rare — only large institutions could afford them.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="tl-item">
    <div class="tl-year">1992</div>
    <div class="tl-title">Computer Education Begins in Schools</div>
    <div class="tl-desc">The Government of Nepal introduced computer science as a subject in secondary schools (Grade 9-10). This was a landmark step in making computer literacy accessible to Nepali students across the country.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="tl-item">
    <div class="tl-year">1993</div>
    <div class="tl-title">First Private IT Companies</div>
    <div class="tl-desc">Private computer training institutes and IT companies emerged in Kathmandu. Companies like Mercantile Communications and others began selling personal computers and providing IT training to the public.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="tl-item">
    <div class="tl-year">1994</div>
    <div class="tl-title">Internet Arrives in Nepal</div>
    <div class="tl-desc"><strong>Nepal Telecom</strong> introduced the first internet service in Nepal in 1994, making Nepal one of the early South Asian countries with internet connectivity. Initially limited to dial-up connections for institutions.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="tl-item">
    <div class="tl-year">1999</div>
    <div class="tl-title">IT Policy of Nepal</div>
    <div class="tl-desc">The Government of Nepal formulated the first <strong>National Information Technology Policy 1999</strong>, recognising ICT as a priority sector for national development. This set the foundation for Nepal's digital future.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="tl-item">
    <div class="tl-year">2000s</div>
    <div class="tl-title">Internet Cafés &amp; Computer Boom</div>
    <div class="tl-desc">Internet cafés (cyber cafés) spread across Nepal's cities and towns, making internet access affordable for ordinary citizens. Personal computer prices dropped significantly — middle-class families began buying PCs. Computer education centres flourished in every district.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="tl-item">
    <div class="tl-year">2007</div>
    <div class="tl-title">Digital Nepal Initiatives</div>
    <div class="tl-desc">Government of Nepal began <strong>e-governance</strong> initiatives — online tax filing, government websites, digital land records. NTC launched ADSL broadband internet. Computer labs established in government schools through donor programs.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="tl-item">
    <div class="tl-year">2010s</div>
    <div class="tl-title">Mobile Revolution &amp; Smartphones</div>
    <div class="tl-desc">Smartphone adoption surged across Nepal. Mobile internet (3G then 4G) reached even remote districts. Mobile payment apps <strong>eSewa (2009)</strong> and <strong>Khalti</strong> transformed digital finance. Nepal's IT industry grew rapidly with software exports to India, USA and UK.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="tl-item">
    <div class="tl-year">2017</div>
    <div class="tl-title">Digital Nepal Framework</div>
    <div class="tl-desc">The Government launched the <strong>Digital Nepal Framework</strong> — an ambitious plan to digitise 8 sectors: digital foundation, agriculture, health, education, energy, tourism, finance and urban infrastructure. Aimed to make Nepal a digital economy by 2022.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="tl-item">
    <div class="tl-year">2020–Present</div>
    <div class="tl-title">COVID, Online Education &amp; 5G Era</div>
    <div class="tl-desc">COVID-19 (2020) forced rapid adoption of online education and remote work in Nepal. Platforms like Pragyapath, YouTube, Zoom became essential for school students. NTC and Ncell launched <strong>5G trials</strong>. Nepal now has over 40 million mobile internet users. Nagarik App provides digital government services.</div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="cmp-grid">
  <div class="cmp-box blue">
    <div class="cmp-tag">Nepal IT Industry Today</div>
    <h3>💼 Growing Sector</h3>
    <p>Nepal's IT sector employs thousands of software developers. Companies in Kathmandu export software to global clients. IT Parks in Bhaktapur and other cities attract investment. Annual IT exports growing rapidly.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="cmp-box grn">
    <div class="cmp-tag">Internet Penetration</div>
    <h3>🌐 Connected Nepal</h3>
    <p>Over 40 million internet users (2024). Fibre broadband in major cities. 4G coverage in most districts. Remote areas connected via satellite internet (Starlink available in Nepal since 2023).</p>
  </div>
  <div class="cmp-box pur">
    <div class="cmp-tag">Digital Education</div>
    <h3>📚 Computer in Schools</h3>
    <p>Computer labs in most government and private schools. ICT included in National Curriculum (Grades 1–12). OLPC (One Laptop per Child) projects in rural areas. Pragyapath and similar platforms provide free digital education.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="cmp-box amb">
    <div class="cmp-tag">Challenges Remaining</div>
    <h3>⚠️ Digital Divide</h3>
    <p>Rural-urban digital gap: cities have fast fibre; remote hills/mountains rely on slow mobile data. Electricity outages affect computer use. Need for more Nepali-language digital content.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="note">🏆 <strong>Notable achievement:</strong> Nepal was one of the first countries in South Asia to install a computer (1971) — even before many wealthier neighbouring nations. Today, young Nepali developers work for global tech companies including Google, Microsoft and Amazon.</div>

<div class="activity">
  <div class="activity-hd">🎯 Activity: Interview a Senior Person</div>
  <p>Talk to a teacher, parent or grandparent and ask: "Do you remember when you first saw or used a computer? What was it like?" Compare their experience with how you use computers and smartphones today. Write down 3 differences and share with the class.</p>
</div>
</div>

</div><!-- /learn -->

<!-- ═══ SIM ═══ -->
<div class="sec" id="sec-sim">
<div class="card">
<h2>🔬 Interactive: Computer Generation Explorer</h2>
<p style="font-size:13px;color:var(--text2,#444)">Click each generation to see the technology, a famous machine, and its specs.</p>
<div class="ctabs">
  <button class="ctab on active" data-ct="gen1">🔴 1st Gen</button>
  <button class="ctab" data-ct="gen2">🟠 2nd Gen</button>
  <button class="ctab" data-ct="gen3">🔵 3rd Gen</button>
  <button class="ctab" data-ct="gen4">🟢 4th Gen</button>
  <button class="ctab" data-ct="gen5">🟣 5th Gen</button>
</div>
<div class="cpanel on" id="gen1">
  <div class="cmp-grid">
    <div class="cmp-box red"><div class="cmp-tag">Technology</div><h3>Vacuum Tubes</h3><p>Giant glass tubes that controlled electrical signals. 18,000 needed in ENIAC. Got very hot and burned out frequently.</p></div>
    <div class="cmp-box amb"><div class="cmp-tag">Famous Machine</div><h3>ENIAC (1945)</h3><p>Weighed 30 tons · Filled 1800 sq ft · 18,000 vacuum tubes · Could do 5,000 additions per second · Cost $400,000</p></div>
    <div class="cmp-box blue"><div class="cmp-tag">Programming</div><h3>Machine Language Only</h3><p>Programmed using physical switches and punched paper cards. No operating system. Very slow and error-prone to program.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="note">⚠️ ENIAC used as much electricity as a small town and its operators had to wait hours for it to cool down between calculations!</div>
</div>
<div class="cpanel" id="gen2">
  <div class="cmp-grid">
    <div class="cmp-box amb"><div class="cmp-tag">Technology</div><h3>Transistors (1956)</h3><p>Tiny semiconductor devices replaced vacuum tubes. 100× faster, far more reliable, used much less power, and didn't burn out.</p></div>
    <div class="cmp-box blue"><div class="cmp-tag">Famous Machine</div><h3>IBM 1401 (1959)</h3><p>First widely installed business computer · Used magnetic tape · Could process payroll, accounting · Rented for $2,500/month</p></div>
    <div class="cmp-box grn"><div class="cmp-tag">Programming</div><h3>Assembly + COBOL/FORTRAN</h3><p>First high-level languages appeared. COBOL for business, FORTRAN for science. Much easier to program than 1st gen.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="eg">✅ A transistor is the size of your fingernail — vs. a vacuum tube the size of a light bulb!</div>
</div>
<div class="cpanel" id="gen3">
  <div class="cmp-grid">
    <div class="cmp-box blue"><div class="cmp-tag">Technology</div><h3>Integrated Circuits (IC)</h3><p>Hundreds of transistors etched onto a single silicon chip. Smaller, faster, cheaper. Made by Texas Instruments and Fairchild.</p></div>
    <div class="cmp-box pur"><div class="cmp-tag">Famous Machine</div><h3>IBM System/360 (1964)</h3><p>First family of compatible computers · Could run same software on different sized machines · Launched the modern software industry</p></div>
    <div class="cmp-box grn"><div class="cmp-tag">Programming</div><h3>Operating Systems + Time-sharing</h3><p>First real operating systems (UNIX, DOS). Multiple users could share one computer simultaneously for the first time.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="tip">💡 An IC the size of a postage stamp could hold circuits that would have filled an entire room in 1st generation!</div>
</div>
<div class="cpanel" id="gen4">
  <div class="cmp-grid">
    <div class="cmp-box grn"><div class="cmp-tag">Technology</div><h3>Microprocessors (VLSI)</h3><p>Millions of transistors on one chip. Intel 4004 (1971) → entire CPU on one silicon chip. Led to personal computers for everyone.</p></div>
    <div class="cmp-box blue"><div class="cmp-tag">Famous Machines</div><h3>Apple II, IBM PC, Mac</h3><p>Apple II (1977) · IBM PC (1981) · Apple Mac (1984) with first GUI · Computers became affordable for homes and schools</p></div>
    <div class="cmp-box pur"><div class="cmp-tag">Programming</div><h3>GUI + High-Level Languages</h3><p>Windows, icons, mouse interface. C, C++, Basic, Pascal. The Internet and World Wide Web (1989). Smartphones (2007).</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="def">🖥️ Your school computer, smartphone and tablet are all 4th generation computers!</div>
</div>
<div class="cpanel" id="gen5">
  <div class="cmp-grid">
    <div class="cmp-box pur"><div class="cmp-tag">Technology</div><h3>AI + Parallel Processing</h3><p>Artificial Intelligence, Machine Learning, Neural Networks. Quantum computing emerging. Chips with billions of transistors.</p></div>
    <div class="cmp-box teal"><div class="cmp-tag">Examples Today</div><h3>AI Systems</h3><p>ChatGPT, AlphaGo, self-driving cars, voice assistants (Siri, Alexa). Computers that learn and reason like humans.</p></div>
    <div class="cmp-box amb"><div class="cmp-tag">Future Direction</div><h3>Quantum Computers</h3><p>Use quantum bits (qubits) instead of 0s and 1s. Can solve problems that would take classical computers billions of years.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="warn">🔮 Quantum computers may one day crack today's encryption — requiring completely new security systems!</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="card">
<h2>🖥️ Simulation: Binary Number Counter</h2>
<p style="font-size:13px;color:var(--text2,#444)">All computers store information using binary (0s and 1s). Click the bits below to toggle them ON/OFF and see the decimal value!</p>
<div class="sim-box">
  <div class="sim-title">Binary ↔ Decimal Converter</div>
  <div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--text3,#888);font-family:var(--mono);text-align:center;margin-bottom:6px">Position values: 128 · 64 · 32 · 16 · 8 · 4 · 2 · 1</div>
  <div class="bit-row" id="bit-row" style="justify-content:center">
    <div class="bit" data-pos="7">0</div><div class="bit" data-pos="6">0</div>
    <div class="bit" data-pos="5">0</div><div class="bit" data-pos="4">0</div>
    <div class="bit" data-pos="3">0</div><div class="bit" data-pos="2">0</div>
    <div class="bit" data-pos="1">0</div><div class="bit" data-pos="0">0</div>
  </div>
