<!-- 1-MARK PANEL -->
<div class="qna-mark-panel on" id="qna-panel-1m">
  
    <div class="qna-group-head">
      <div class="qna-group-icon gi-1m">🔵</div>
      <div>
        <div class="qna-group-title">Very Short Questions — 1 Mark</div>
        <div class="qna-group-sub">Fill in the blank · MCQ · True/False · One-word answers</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  
    <div class="qna-card m1">
      <div class="qna-q" onclick="qnaToggle(this)">
        <div class="qna-q-num">8</div>
        <div class="qna-q-body">
          <div class="qna-badge">1 Mark · Fill in the Blank</div>
          <div class="qna-q-en">Nepal's first computer, an IBM 1401, was installed at the _______ in 1971.</div>
          <div class="qna-q-np">नेपालको पहिलो कम्प्युटर IBM 1401 सन् १९७१ मा _______ मा जडान गरिएको थियो।</div>
        </div>
        <div class="qna-chevron">▼</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qna-a"><div class="qna-a-inner"><p><strong>Central Bureau of Statistics (CBS) / केन्द्रीय तथ्याङ्क विभाग</strong></p><p>Nepal's first computer was an IBM 1401 (a second-generation transistor computer) installed at the Central Bureau of Statistics in Kathmandu in 1971. It was used to process census data, making Nepal one of the earliest countries in South Asia to use a computer for government work.</p></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="qna-card m1">
      <div class="qna-q" onclick="qnaToggle(this)">
        <div class="qna-q-num">12</div>
        <div class="qna-q-body">
          <div class="qna-badge">1 Mark · MCQ</div>
          <div class="qna-q-en">Internet service was first introduced in Nepal in which year?<br>a) 1990 &nbsp; b) 1994 &nbsp; c) 1999 &nbsp; d) 2007</div>
          <div class="qna-q-np">नेपालमा इन्टरनेट सेवा पहिलो पटक कुन सालमा सुरु भयो?<br>a) १९९० &nbsp; b) १९९४ &nbsp; c) १९९९ &nbsp; d) २००७</div>
        </div>
        <div class="qna-chevron">▼</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qna-a"><div class="qna-a-inner"><p><strong>b) 1994</strong></p><p>Nepal Telecom introduced the first internet service in Nepal in 1994, making Nepal one of the early South Asian countries with internet connectivity. Initially available only as dial-up connections for institutions, internet access gradually expanded to the public through the 1990s and 2000s.</p></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="qna-card m1">
      <div class="qna-q" onclick="qnaToggle(this)">
        <div class="qna-q-num">4</div>
        <div class="qna-q-body">
          <div class="qna-badge">1 Mark · Fill in the Blank</div>
          <div class="qna-q-en">ENIAC stands for _______.</div>
          <div class="qna-q-np">ENIAC को पूर्ण रूप _______ हो।</div>
        </div>
        <div class="qna-chevron">▼</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qna-a"><div class="qna-a-inner"><p><strong>Electronic Numerical Integrator And Computer</strong></p><p>ENIAC was built at the University of Pennsylvania between 1943–1945. It was the first general-purpose electronic computer — weighed 30 tons, occupied 1,800 sq ft, and used 18,000 vacuum tubes. It could perform 5,000 additions per second.</p></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="qna-card m1">
      <div class="qna-q" onclick="qnaToggle(this)">
        <div class="qna-q-num">11</div>
        <div class="qna-q-body">
          <div class="qna-badge">1 Mark · One Word</div>
          <div class="qna-q-en">Name the first general-purpose electronic computer built in 1945.</div>
          <div class="qna-q-np">सन् १९४५ मा निर्मित पहिलो सर्वउद्देश्यीय इलेक्ट्रोनिक कम्प्युटरको नाम लेख्नुहोस्।</div>
        </div>
        <div class="qna-chevron">▼</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qna-a"><div class="qna-a-inner"><p><strong>ENIAC</strong> (Electronic Numerical Integrator And Computer)</p><p>Built at the University of Pennsylvania by J. Presper Eckert and John Mauchly. Completed in 1945, it was the first Turing-complete, general-purpose electronic computer. It filled an entire room and was used by the US Army for ballistic trajectory calculations.</p></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="qna-card m1">
      <div class="qna-q" onclick="qnaToggle(this)">
        <div class="qna-q-num">9</div>
        <div class="qna-q-body">
          <div class="qna-badge">1 Mark · MCQ</div>
          <div class="qna-q-en">Which technology characterises FIFTH generation computers?<br>a) Vacuum tubes &nbsp; b) Transistors &nbsp; c) Integrated Circuits &nbsp; d) Artificial Intelligence</div>
          <div class="qna-q-np">पाँचौँ पुस्ताका कम्प्युटरको विशेषता कुन प्रविधि हो?<br>a) भ्याकुम ट्युब &nbsp; b) ट्रान्जिस्टर &nbsp; c) इन्टिग्रेटेड सर्किट &nbsp; d) कृत्रिम बुद्धिमत्ता</div>
        </div>
        <div class="qna-chevron">▼</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qna-a"><div class="qna-a-inner"><p><strong>d) Artificial Intelligence (AI)</strong></p><p>Fifth generation computers (present and future) are based on AI, machine learning, neural networks, natural language processing and parallel processing. They aim to create machines that can think and reason. Japan's FGCS project pioneered this. Examples: ChatGPT, AlphaGo, self-driving cars.</p></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="qna-card m1">
      <div class="qna-q" onclick="qnaToggle(this)">
        <div class="qna-q-num">5</div>
        <div class="qna-q-body">
          <div class="qna-badge">1 Mark · MCQ</div>
          <div class="qna-q-en">Who is known as the "Father of the Computer"?<br>a) Alan Turing &nbsp; b) John von Neumann &nbsp; c) Charles Babbage &nbsp; d) Grace Hopper</div>
          <div class="qna-q-np">"कम्प्युटरका पिता" को रूपमा कसलाई चिनिन्छ?<br>a) एलन ट्युरिङ &nbsp; b) जोन भन न्युम्यान &nbsp; c) चार्ल्स ब्याबेज &nbsp; d) ग्रेस हपर</div>
        </div>
        <div class="qna-chevron">▼</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qna-a"><div class="qna-a-inner"><p><strong>c) Charles Babbage (1791–1871)</strong></p><p>Charles Babbage designed the Difference Engine (1822) and Analytical Engine (1837) — mechanical computing machines that laid the conceptual foundation of all modern computers. He is therefore called the "Father of the Computer."</p></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="qna-card m1">
      <div class="qna-q" onclick="qnaToggle(this)">
        <div class="qna-q-num">10</div>
        <div class="qna-q-body">
          <div class="qna-badge">1 Mark · MCQ</div>
          <div class="qna-q-en">Napier's Bones (1617) were primarily used for:<br>a) Addition and subtraction &nbsp; b) Multiplication and division &nbsp; c) Memory storage &nbsp; d) Binary computation</div>
          <div class="qna-q-np">नेपियरको हड्डी (१६१७) मुख्यतः कसका लागि प्रयोग गरिन्थ्यो?<br>a) जोड र घटाउ &nbsp; b) गुणा र भाग &nbsp; c) मेमोरी भण्डारण &nbsp; d) बाइनरी गणना</div>
        </div>
        <div class="qna-chevron">▼</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qna-a"><div class="qna-a-inner"><p><strong>b) Multiplication and division</strong></p><p>John Napier invented Napier's Bones in 1617 — a set of numbered rods (originally made from bone or ivory) that allowed rapid multiplication and division of large numbers. They were also used to extract square roots and cube roots. Napier also invented logarithms, which revolutionised mathematics.</p></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="qna-card m1">
      <div class="qna-q" onclick="qnaToggle(this)">
        <div class="qna-q-num">2</div>
        <div class="qna-q-body">
          <div class="qna-badge">1 Mark · MCQ</div>
          <div class="qna-q-en">Who invented the Pascaline (1642)?<br>a) Charles Babbage &nbsp; b) Blaise Pascal &nbsp; c) Ada Lovelace &nbsp; d) Alan Turing</div>
          <div class="qna-q-np">पास्कलिन (१६४२) कसले आविष्कार गरे?<br>a) चार्ल्स ब्याबेज &nbsp; b) ब्लेज पास्कल &nbsp; c) एडा लभलेस &nbsp; d) एलन ट्युरिङ</div>
        </div>
        <div class="qna-chevron">▼</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qna-a"><div class="qna-a-inner"><p><strong>b) Blaise Pascal</strong></p><p>Blaise Pascal (French mathematician, 1623–1662) invented the Pascaline in 1642 at age 19 to help his father with tax calculations. It was a mechanical adding machine using gears and wheels that could add and subtract numbers automatically.</p></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="qna-card m1">
      <div class="qna-q" onclick="qnaToggle(this)">
        <div class="qna-q-num">1</div>
        <div class="qna-q-body">
          <div class="qna-badge">1 Mark · Fill in the Blank</div>
          <div class="qna-q-en">The earliest computing tool, the _______, was invented in China around 2400 BC.</div>
          <div class="qna-q-np">सबैभन्दा पुरानो कम्प्युटिङ उपकरण _______ चीनमा सन् २४०० ईसापूर्वतिर आविष्कार भएको थियो।</div>
        </div>
        <div class="qna-chevron">▼</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qna-a"><div class="qna-a-inner"><p><strong>Abacus (अबाकस)</strong></p><p>The Abacus is the world's oldest computing device, originating in China ~2400 BC. It uses rows of beads on rods to perform arithmetic (addition, subtraction, multiplication, division). Still used today in some parts of Asia for mental arithmetic training.</p></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="qna-card m1">
      <div class="qna-q" onclick="qnaToggle(this)">
        <div class="qna-q-num">7</div>
        <div class="qna-q-body">
          <div class="qna-badge">1 Mark · MCQ</div>
          <div class="qna-q-en">The WWW (World Wide Web) was invented in which year?<br>a) 1969 &nbsp; b) 1981 &nbsp; c) 1989 &nbsp; d) 1995</div>
          <div class="qna-q-np">WWW (वर्ल्ड वाइड वेब) कुन सालमा आविष्कार भयो?<br>a) १९६९ &nbsp; b) १९८१ &nbsp; c) १९८९ &nbsp; d) १९९५</div>
        </div>
        <div class="qna-chevron">▼</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qna-a"><div class="qna-a-inner"><p><strong>c) 1989</strong></p><p>Tim Berners-Lee invented the World Wide Web in 1989 at CERN (European Organisation for Nuclear Research) in Switzerland. He proposed it as an information management system using hypertext links. The first website went live in 1991.</p></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="qna-card m1">
      <div class="qna-q" onclick="qnaToggle(this)">
        <div class="qna-q-num">3</div>
        <div class="qna-q-body">
          <div class="qna-badge">1 Mark · MCQ</div>
          <div class="qna-q-en">Which generation of computers used transistors?<br>a) First &nbsp; b) Second &nbsp; c) Third &nbsp; d) Fourth</div>
          <div class="qna-q-np">कुन पुस्ताको कम्प्युटरमा ट्रान्जिस्टर प्रयोग भयो?<br>a) पहिलो &nbsp; b) दोस्रो &nbsp; c) तेस्रो &nbsp; d) चौथो</div>
        </div>
        <div class="qna-chevron">▼</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qna-a"><div class="qna-a-inner"><p><strong>b) Second Generation (1956–1963)</strong></p><p>Second generation computers replaced large, unreliable vacuum tubes with transistors — smaller, faster, more reliable semiconductor devices. They consumed far less power and generated less heat. Examples: IBM 1401, IBM 7090.</p></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="qna-card m1">
      <div class="qna-q" onclick="qnaToggle(this)">
        <div class="qna-q-num">6</div>
        <div class="qna-q-body">
          <div class="qna-badge">1 Mark · True / False</div>
          <div class="qna-q-en">State True or False: Ada Lovelace is considered the world's first computer programmer.</div>
          <div class="qna-q-np">सत्य वा असत्य बताउनुहोस्: एडा लभलेसलाई संसारकी पहिलो कम्प्युटर प्रोग्रामर मानिन्छ।</div>
        </div>
        <div class="qna-chevron">▼</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qna-a"><div class="qna-a-inner"><p><strong>True (सत्य)</strong></p><p>Ada Lovelace (1815–1852) wrote the first algorithm intended to be executed by a machine — for Charles Babbage's Analytical Engine in 1843. This algorithm was designed to compute Bernoulli numbers, making her the world's first programmer, over 100 years before modern computers existed.</p></div></div>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- 2-MARK PANEL -->
<div class="qna-mark-panel" id="qna-panel-2m">
  
    <div class="qna-group-head">
      <div class="qna-group-icon gi-2m">🟠</div>
      <div>
        <div class="qna-group-title">Short Answer &amp; Reason Questions — 2 Marks</div>
        <div class="qna-group-sub">Short answers · Give reason · Differentiate</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  
    <div class="qna-card m2">
      <div class="qna-q" onclick="qnaToggle(this)">
        <div class="qna-q-num">2</div>
        <div class="qna-q-body">
          <div class="qna-badge">2 Marks · Short Answer</div>
          <div class="qna-q-en">What is the difference between the Difference Engine and the Analytical Engine designed by Charles Babbage?</div>
          <div class="qna-q-np">चार्ल्स ब्याबेजले डिजाइन गरेको डिफरेन्स इन्जिन र एनालिटिकल इन्जिनमा के फरक छ?</div>
        </div>
        <div class="qna-chevron">▼</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qna-a"><div class="qna-a-inner"><table><tbody><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Difference Engine (1822)</th><th>Analytical Engine (1837)</th></tr><tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Compute mathematical tables only (specific task)</td><td>General-purpose — could perform any calculation</td></tr><tr><td>Programmable?</td><td>No — fixed function</td><td>Yes — via punched cards</td></tr><tr><td>Components</td><td>Gears and columns</td><td>Mill (CPU), Store (memory), Input, Output</td></tr><tr><td>Modern equivalent</td><td>A calculator</td><td>A full computer</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="qna-card m2">
      <div class="qna-q" onclick="qnaToggle(this)">
        <div class="qna-q-num">3</div>
        <div class="qna-q-body">
          <div class="qna-badge">2 Marks · Give Reason</div>
          <div class="qna-q-en">Give reason: Second generation computers were much better than first generation computers.</div>
          <div class="qna-q-np">कारण दिनुहोस्: दोस्रो पुस्ताका कम्प्युटरहरू पहिलो पुस्ताका भन्दा धेरै राम्रा थिए।</div>
        </div>
        <div class="qna-chevron">▼</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qna-a"><div class="qna-a-inner"><p>Second generation computers (1956–1963) replaced vacuum tubes with <strong>transistors</strong>, which gave several major advantages:</p><ul><li><strong>Smaller size:</strong> Transistors are fingernail-sized vs. light-bulb-sized vacuum tubes → computers shrank from room-sized to cabinet-sized</li><li><strong>Faster speed:</strong> ~100× faster processing</li><li><strong>More reliable:</strong> Transistors rarely burned out; vacuum tubes failed constantly</li><li><strong>Less power:</strong> Used far less electricity and generated much less heat</li><li><strong>Better programming:</strong> High-level languages (COBOL, FORTRAN) appeared</li></ul></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="qna-card m2">
      <div class="qna-q" onclick="qnaToggle(this)">
        <div class="qna-q-num">5</div>
        <div class="qna-q-body">
          <div class="qna-badge">2 Marks · Short Answer</div>
          <div class="qna-q-en">What is the von Neumann Architecture? Why is it important?</div>
          <div class="qna-q-np">भन न्युम्यान आर्किटेक्चर भनेको के हो? यो किन महत्त्वपूर्ण छ?</div>
        </div>
        <div class="qna-chevron">▼</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qna-a"><div class="qna-a-inner"><p>The <strong>von Neumann Architecture</strong> (1945), proposed by mathematician John von Neumann, is a computer design model with four key components:</p><ul><li><strong>CPU</strong> — processes instructions</li><li><strong>Memory (RAM)</strong> — stores both programs AND data in the same place</li><li><strong>Input/Output</strong> — communicates with the outside world</li><li><strong>Control Unit</strong> — fetches, decodes and executes instructions in sequence</li></ul><p><strong>Importance:</strong> ALL modern computers — from smartphones to supercomputers — use von Neumann Architecture. The critical innovation was the <em>stored-program concept</em>: programs are stored in memory just like data, meaning the computer can be reprogrammed without physical rewiring.</p></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="qna-card m2">
      <div class="qna-q" onclick="qnaToggle(this)">
        <div class="qna-q-num">7</div>
        <div class="qna-q-body">
          <div class="qna-badge">2 Marks · Give Reason</div>
          <div class="qna-q-en">Give reason: Computers became affordable for ordinary people only in the fourth generation.</div>
          <div class="qna-q-np">कारण दिनुहोस्: चौथो पुस्तामा मात्र कम्प्युटर सामान्य मानिसहरूका लागि किफायती भयो।</div>
        </div>
        <div class="qna-chevron">▼</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qna-a"><div class="qna-a-inner"><p>Fourth generation computers (1971–present) use <strong>microprocessors</strong> — entire CPUs etched onto a single tiny silicon chip using Very Large Scale Integration (VLSI) technology.</p><p><strong>Why this made computers affordable:</strong></p><ul><li>Manufacturing chips is cheap at scale — millions of transistors on a chip costing cents</li><li>Computers shrank from cabinet-sized to desktop-sized</li><li>Intel 4004 (1971) → Apple II (1977) → IBM PC (1981) → prices dropped from millions to hundreds of dollars</li><li>Mass production of standardised components drove costs down further each year</li><li>By the 1980s, individuals and families could own a personal computer for the first time</li></ul></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="qna-card m2">
      <div class="qna-q" onclick="qnaToggle(this)">
        <div class="qna-q-num">6</div>
        <div class="qna-q-body">
          <div class="qna-badge">2 Marks · Short Answer</div>
          <div class="qna-q-en">What was the UNIVAC-I and why was it historically significant?</div>
          <div class="qna-q-np">UNIVAC-I के थियो र यो ऐतिहासिक रूपमा किन महत्त्वपूर्ण थियो?</div>
        </div>
        <div class="qna-chevron">▼</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qna-a"><div class="qna-a-inner"><p><strong>UNIVAC-I</strong> (Universal Automatic Computer, 1951) was the first commercially produced computer in the United States, designed by J. Presper Eckert and John Mauchly.</p><p><strong>Historical significance:</strong></p><ul><li>First computer sold commercially to a business — delivered to the US Census Bureau in 1951</li><li>First computer used to predict a US Presidential election result (1952) — correctly predicted Eisenhower's victory</li><li>Used magnetic tape instead of punched cards — much faster data storage</li><li>Proved computers were practical tools for business and government, not just academic experiments</li></ul></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="qna-card m2">
      <div class="qna-q" onclick="qnaToggle(this)">
        <div class="qna-q-num">10</div>
        <div class="qna-q-body">
          <div class="qna-badge">2 Marks · Differentiate</div>
          <div class="qna-q-en">Differentiate between Analog and Digital computers.</div>
          <div class="qna-q-np">एनालग र डिजिटल कम्प्युटरबीच भिन्नता लेख्नुहोस्।</div>
        </div>
        <div class="qna-chevron">▼</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qna-a"><div class="qna-a-inner"><table><tbody><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Analog Computer</th><th>Digital Computer</th></tr><tr><td>Data type</td><td>Continuous signals (voltage, current, temperature)</td><td>Discrete values — only 0s and 1s</td></tr><tr><td>Accuracy</td><td>Less precise — approximate results</td><td>Very precise — exact results</td></tr><tr><td>Speed</td><td>Real-time but not programmable</td><td>Very fast and fully programmable</td></tr><tr><td>Examples</td><td>Speedometer, thermometer, oscilloscope</td><td>PC, laptop, smartphone, tablet</td></tr><tr><td>Use today</td><td>Rare — mostly industrial sensors</td><td>Universal — almost all modern computers</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="qna-card m2">
      <div class="qna-q" onclick="qnaToggle(this)">
        <div class="qna-q-num">8</div>
        <div class="qna-q-body">
          <div class="qna-badge">2 Marks · Short Answer</div>
          <div class="qna-q-en">What is the binary number system and why do computers use it?</div>
          <div class="qna-q-np">बाइनरी नम्बर सिस्टम भनेको के हो र कम्प्युटरले यसलाई किन प्रयोग गर्छ?</div>
        </div>
        <div class="qna-chevron">▼</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qna-a"><div class="qna-a-inner"><p>The <strong>binary number system</strong> uses only two digits — <strong>0</strong> and <strong>1</strong> (bits). Every number, letter, image and video stored in a computer is represented as a combination of 0s and 1s.</p><p><strong>Why computers use binary:</strong></p><ul><li>Electronic circuits have only two reliable states: <strong>OFF</strong> (no voltage = 0) and <strong>ON</strong> (voltage = 1)</li><li>This maps perfectly to binary — making hardware extremely simple, reliable and fast</li><li>Gottfried Leibniz invented binary in 1671; George Boole formalised Boolean algebra (AND/OR/NOT operations) in 1854 — the mathematical foundation of all computing</li></ul><p>Example: Letter "A" = decimal 65 = binary 01000001</p></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="qna-card m2">
      <div class="qna-q" onclick="qnaToggle(this)">
        <div class="qna-q-num">1</div>
        <div class="qna-q-body">
          <div class="qna-badge">2 Marks · Short Answer</div>
          <div class="qna-q-en">What is the Jacquard Loom and why is it significant in the history of computing?</div>
          <div class="qna-q-np">ज्याकार्ड लुम भनेको के हो र कम्प्युटिङको इतिहासमा यसको के महत्त्व छ?</div>
        </div>
        <div class="qna-chevron">▼</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qna-a"><div class="qna-a-inner"><p>The <strong>Jacquard Loom</strong> (1801) was a mechanical weaving loom invented by Joseph Marie Jacquard in France. It used <strong>punched cards</strong> to control the pattern of weaving — each card had holes that guided the loom's needles.</p><p><strong>Significance:</strong> It was the world's first programmable machine — the idea of using punched cards to store and execute instructions directly inspired Charles Babbage's Analytical Engine and later computer punched-card input systems used well into the 1970s.</p></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="qna-card m2">
      <div class="qna-q" onclick="qnaToggle(this)">
        <div class="qna-q-num">4</div>
        <div class="qna-q-body">
          <div class="qna-badge">2 Marks · Short Answer</div>
          <div class="qna-q-en">Who was Grace Hopper and what were her two most important contributions to computing?</div>
          <div class="qna-q-np">ग्रेस हपर को थिइन् र कम्प्युटिङमा उनका दुईवटा महत्त्वपूर्ण योगदानहरू के थिए?</div>
        </div>
        <div class="qna-chevron">▼</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qna-a"><div class="qna-a-inner"><p><strong>Grace Hopper (1906–1992)</strong> was an American computer scientist and US Navy Rear Admiral who made fundamental contributions to early computing:</p><p><strong>1. First Compiler (1952):</strong> Created the first compiler — a program that automatically translates human-readable code into machine language. This made programming vastly easier and is the foundation of all modern programming languages.</p><p><strong>2. Coined "Debugging" (1947):</strong> When a relay in the Harvard Mark II computer failed due to a trapped moth, she taped the moth in the logbook and wrote "First actual case of bug being found." The word "debugging" for fixing software errors originates from this incident.</p></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="qna-card m2">
      <div class="qna-q" onclick="qnaToggle(this)">
        <div class="qna-q-num">9</div>
        <div class="qna-q-body">
          <div class="qna-badge">2 Marks · Short Answer</div>
          <div class="qna-q-en">Write two major contributions of Nepal in the field of ICT development.</div>
          <div class="qna-q-np">ICT विकासको क्षेत्रमा नेपालका दुईवटा प्रमुख योगदानहरू लेख्नुहोस्।</div>
        </div>
        <div class="qna-chevron">▼</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qna-a"><div class="qna-a-inner"><p><strong>1. Early adoption of computing (1971):</strong> Nepal installed its first computer (IBM 1401) at the Central Bureau of Statistics in 1971 — making it one of the earliest countries in South Asia to use computing for government administration. Nepal introduced computer education in schools as early as 1992.</p><p><strong>2. Digital financial innovation:</strong> Nepal developed eSewa (2009), one of South Asia's earliest mobile payment platforms, enabling millions of Nepalis to make digital transactions without a bank account. This innovation preceded similar platforms in many larger countries and helped bridge the financial access gap in remote areas of Nepal.</p></div></div>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- 4-MARK PANEL -->
<div class="qna-mark-panel" id="qna-panel-4m">
  
    <div class="qna-group-head">
      <div class="qna-group-icon gi-4m">🟢</div>
      <div>
        <div class="qna-group-title">Long Answer Questions — 4 Marks</div>
        <div class="qna-group-sub">Essay answers · Detailed comparisons · Nepal history</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  
    <div class="qna-card m4">
      <div class="qna-q" onclick="qnaToggle(this)">
        <div class="qna-q-num">2</div>
        <div class="qna-q-body">
          <div class="qna-badge">4 Marks · Long Answer</div>
          <div class="qna-q-en">Write a detailed account of the history of computers in Nepal from 1971 to the present day.</div>
          <div class="qna-q-np">सन् १९७१ देखि आजसम्म नेपालमा कम्प्युटरको इतिहासको विस्तृत विवरण लेख्नुहोस्।</div>
        </div>
        <div class="qna-chevron">▼</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qna-a"><div class="qna-a-inner"><p>Nepal's computing history spans over five decades of steady progress:</p><p><strong>1971 — First Computer:</strong> IBM 1401 installed at the Central Bureau of Statistics for census data processing. Nepal was among South Asia's earliest computer users.</p><p><strong>1975:</strong> Tribhuvan University acquired computers for academic research. Computer education began at the university level.</p><p><strong>1980s:</strong> Banks and government ministries computerised records. Computers were rare and expensive — only large institutions could afford them.</p><p><strong>1992:</strong> Computer science introduced as a school subject (Grades 9–10) — a landmark for national computer literacy.</p><p><strong>1994:</strong> Nepal Telecom launched internet services — Nepal became one of South Asia's earliest countries with internet access.</p><p><strong>1999:</strong> Government formulated Nepal's first National IT Policy, establishing ICT as a development priority.</p><p><strong>2000s:</strong> Internet cafés spread across cities and towns; personal computer prices fell; computer training institutes opened in every district.</p><p><strong>2007:</strong> e-governance initiatives — online tax filing, digital land records, government websites. ADSL broadband launched.</p><p><strong>2009–2010s:</strong> eSewa mobile payment launched (2009) — a pioneering digital finance platform. Smartphone adoption surged. 3G then 4G mobile internet reached most districts.</p><p><strong>2017:</strong> Digital Nepal Framework launched — ambitious plan to digitise agriculture, health, education, energy, tourism and finance.</p><p><strong>2020–present:</strong> COVID-19 accelerated online education (Pragyapath, YouTube, Zoom). Over 40 million internet users. Starlink satellite internet available (2023). 5G trials underway.</p><p><strong>Remaining challenges:</strong> Rural-urban digital divide, electricity reliability, insufficient Nepali-language digital content, affordability of devices in remote areas.</p></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="qna-card m4">
      <div class="qna-q" onclick="qnaToggle(this)">
        <div class="qna-q-num">3</div>
        <div class="qna-q-body">
          <div class="qna-badge">4 Marks · Long Answer</div>
          <div class="qna-q-en">Explain the contributions of Charles Babbage and Ada Lovelace to the history of computing. Why are they both considered pioneers?</div>
          <div class="qna-q-np">कम्प्युटिङको इतिहासमा चार्ल्स ब्याबेज र एडा लभलेसको योगदान व्याख्या गर्नुहोस्। उनीहरू दुवैलाई अग्रदूत किन मानिन्छ?</div>
        </div>
        <div class="qna-chevron">▼</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qna-a"><div class="qna-a-inner"><p><strong>Charles Babbage (1791–1871) — "Father of the Computer":</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Difference Engine (1822):</strong> Designed a mechanical steam-powered calculator to compute polynomial mathematical tables automatically. Funded by the British government. Proved the principle of mechanical computation.</li><li><strong>Analytical Engine (1837):</strong> A far more ambitious design — the first concept of a general-purpose programmable computer. It had: a "Mill" (equivalent to today's CPU), a "Store" (memory that could hold 1,000 50-digit numbers), punched card input, and printed output. Babbage's Analytical Engine contained ALL the logical elements of a modern computer — over 150 years before electronic computers existed.</li><li>Never fully built in his lifetime due to funding and technical limitations, but later reconstructed by the London Science Museum (1991), proving his designs were correct.</li></ul><p><strong>Ada Lovelace (1815–1852) — "World's First Programmer":</strong></p><ul><li>Daughter of poet Lord Byron; studied mathematics under Mary Somerville.</li><li>In 1843, she translated an Italian article about Babbage's Analytical Engine and added her own extensive notes — three times longer than the original article.</li><li>Her Note G contained the first published algorithm intended for machine execution — a method to compute Bernoulli numbers. This is recognised as the first computer program.</li><li>She also predicted that the Analytical Engine could compose music and handle any symbolic information, not just numbers — a revolutionary vision of what computers could do.</li></ul><p><strong>Why they are pioneers:</strong> Together, Babbage and Lovelace conceptualised hardware (programmable mechanical computer) and software (algorithm) — the two pillars of computing — nearly a century before electronics made it practical.</p></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="qna-card m4">
      <div class="qna-q" onclick="qnaToggle(this)">
        <div class="qna-q-num">1</div>
        <div class="qna-q-body">
          <div class="qna-badge">4 Marks · Long Answer</div>
          <div class="qna-q-en">Describe the five generations of computers. Include the technology used, notable machines, and key characteristics of each generation.</div>
          <div class="qna-q-np">कम्प्युटरका पाँच पुस्ताहरूको वर्णन गर्नुहोस्। प्रत्येक पुस्तामा प्रयोग गरिएको प्रविधि, उल्लेखनीय मेसिन र मुख्य विशेषताहरू समावेश गर्नुहोस्।</div>
        </div>
        <div class="qna-chevron">▼</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qna-a"><div class="qna-a-inner"><p>Computers are classified into <strong>five generations</strong> based on the technology used:</p><table><tbody><tr><th>Generation</th><th>Period</th><th>Technology</th><th>Key Feature</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td><strong>1st</strong></td><td>1940s–1956</td><td>Vacuum tubes</td><td>Room-sized, very hot, punched card input, machine language only</td><td>ENIAC, UNIVAC-I</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2nd</strong></td><td>1956–1963</td><td>Transistors</td><td>Smaller, faster, more reliable, assembly + high-level languages (COBOL, FORTRAN)</td><td>IBM 1401, IBM 7090</td></tr><tr><td><strong>3rd</strong></td><td>1964–1971</td><td>Integrated Circuits (IC)</td><td>Much smaller chips, operating systems, multi-user time-sharing, nanosecond speed</td><td>IBM System/360</td></tr><tr><td><strong>4th</strong></td><td>1971–present</td><td>Microprocessors (VLSI)</td><td>Personal computers, GUI, internet, billions of transistors on one chip</td><td>Apple Mac, IBM PC</td></tr><tr><td><strong>5th</strong></td><td>Present–future</td><td>AI / Quantum</td><td>Machine learning, neural networks, natural language processing, parallel processing</td><td>ChatGPT, quantum computers</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Trend across generations:</strong> Each generation brought computers that were faster ↑, smaller ↓, cheaper ↓, more energy-efficient ↑, and more accessible to ordinary people ↑.</p></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="qna-card m4">
      <div class="qna-q" onclick="qnaToggle(this)">
        <div class="qna-q-num">4</div>
        <div class="qna-q-body">
          <div class="qna-badge">4 Marks · Long Answer</div>
          <div class="qna-q-en">Compare the first and fourth generations of computers in detail, covering technology, size, speed, cost, programming, and use.</div>
          <div class="qna-q-np">पहिलो र चौथो पुस्ताका कम्प्युटरहरूको विस्तृत तुलना गर्नुहोस् — प्रविधि, आकार, गति, लागत, प्रोग्रामिङ र प्रयोग समावेश गर्नुहोस्।</div>
        </div>
        <div class="qna-chevron">▼</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qna-a"><div class="qna-a-inner"><table><tbody><tr><th>Feature</th><th>First Generation (1940s–1956)</th><th>Fourth Generation (1971–present)</th></tr><tr><td>Core technology</td><td>Vacuum tubes (18,000 in ENIAC)</td><td>Microprocessors (VLSI — billions of transistors on one chip)</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>Room-sized (1,800 sq ft for ENIAC)</td><td>Fits in a pocket (smartphone)</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>~30,000 kg</td><td>~170 g (smartphone)</td></tr><tr><td>Speed</td><td>~5,000 operations per second</td><td>Billions per second (GHz)</td></tr><tr><td>Power use</td><td>150,000 watts</td><td>5–100 watts</td></tr><tr><td>Reliability</td><td>Poor — vacuum tubes burned out constantly</td><td>Very high — no moving parts in chips</td></tr><tr><td>Programming</td><td>Machine language only (binary); physical switches and punched cards</td><td>High-level languages (Python, Java, C); GUI; touch interfaces</td></tr><tr><td>Cost</td><td>$400,000+ (1940s dollars)</td><td>$200–$2,000</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>Punched cards; no permanent storage</td><td>256 GB+ SSD; cloud storage</td></tr><tr><td>Users</td><td>Only trained engineers; 1 computer for entire institution</td><td>Billions of ordinary people worldwide</td></tr><tr><td>Connectivity</td><td>None — completely isolated machines</td><td>Internet, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, 5G</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> In just 80 years, computers evolved from a 30-ton room-filling machine affordable only to governments, to a 170-gram pocket device used by over 4 billion people. This is arguably the fastest technological transformation in human history.</p></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="qna-card m4">
      <div class="qna-q" onclick="qnaToggle(this)">
        <div class="qna-q-num">5</div>
        <div class="qna-q-body">
          <div class="qna-badge">4 Marks · Long Answer</div>
          <div class="qna-q-en">Explain the contributions of Alan Turing and John von Neumann to the history of computing.</div>
          <div class="qna-q-np">कम्प्युटिङको इतिहासमा एलन ट्युरिङ र जोन भन न्युम्यानको योगदान व्याख्या गर्नुहोस्।</div>
        </div>
        <div class="qna-chevron">▼</div>
      </div>
      <div class="qna-a"><div class="qna-a-inner"><p><strong>Alan Turing (1912–1954):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Turing Machine (1936):</strong> Proposed a theoretical mathematical model of computation — a device that reads and writes symbols on an infinite tape according to rules. This abstract model defines what is "computable" and is the theoretical foundation of all modern computers and computer science.</li><li><strong>Codebreaking in WW2 (1939–1945):</strong> Led the team at Bletchley Park that cracked the German Enigma cipher using the "Bombe" machine, shortening WW2 by an estimated 2–4 years and saving millions of lives.</li><li><strong>Turing Test (1950):</strong> Proposed a test for machine intelligence — if a human cannot distinguish a machine's responses from a human's, the machine can be considered "intelligent." This is the foundation of Artificial Intelligence research.</li><li>Called the "Father of Computer Science and Artificial Intelligence."</li></ul><p><strong>John von Neumann (1903–1957):</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Von Neumann Architecture (1945):</strong> Described the stored-program computer model — a computer where programs are stored in the same memory as data. This revolutionary idea meant computers could be reprogrammed without physical rewiring.</li><li>His architecture (CPU + Memory + I/O + sequential instruction execution) is used by ALL computers today — from microcontrollers to supercomputers.</li><li>Contributed to the Manhattan Project, game theory, quantum mechanics, and numerical analysis — but his computer architecture is his most enduring legacy.</li></ul><p><strong>Combined legacy:</strong> Turing defined WHAT a computer can do theoretically; von Neumann defined HOW to build one practically. Together, their work forms the complete intellectual foundation of the computer age.</p></div></div>
    </div>
</div>

</div><!-- /qna -->

</div><!-- /wrap -->

